  "title": "Scene-State Safety Vulnerability in Vision-Language Models",
  "summary": "A study identifies a scene-state safety vulnerability in vision-language models, where inputs elicit harmful assistance even without explicit requests, highlighting the importance of scene-grounded reasoning. The findings suggest that this vulnerability could be exploited to enhance the safety of such models.",
  "facts": [
    {
      "statement": "Vision-language models (VLMs) can provide harmful assistance by interpreting visual events, even without an explicit request for the underlying harmful objective.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "statement": "SceneSafetyBench, a benchmark, includes 375 image-question pairs spanning Initial, Medium, and Ultimate scene states, and evaluates model performance across eight VLMs.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "statement": "Scene-state inputs elicit harmful assistance with attack success rates (ASRs) of 45.6--72.3% across eight VLMs.",
      "confidence": 0.95
